    <!-- ===== Help ===== -->
    <section class="jim-admin-panel" data-panel="help">
        <h3>Admin guide</h3>
        <div class="jim-admin-help">
            <h4>Global chat mode</h4>
            <p><strong>Allow everyone</strong> is the default: any active Jira user can find and message any other user.
               <strong>Restricted by policy</strong> switches to a whitelist model — a user can only search/start/receive chat when an ALLOW policy matches and no higher-priority DENY matches.
               <strong>Disabled</strong> turns chat off entirely: user search returns nothing and starting or sending direct messages is rejected by the server.</p>

            <h4>How policies are evaluated</h4>
            <ol>
                <li>Only <em>enabled</em> policies whose capability checkbox (search / start / receive) matches the action being checked are considered.</li>
                <li>The policy <em>source</em> must match the acting user (their username, user key, or a group they belong to).</li>
                <li>The policy <em>target</em> must match the other user (username/key, group, or ANY).</li>
                <li>Among matching policies the <strong>highest priority wins</strong>; on a priority tie <strong>DENY overrides ALLOW</strong>.</li>
                <li>If no policy matches, access is <strong>denied</strong> (restricted mode is a whitelist).</li>
            </ol>
            <p>Starting a chat additionally requires the recipient to be allowed to <em>receive</em> chat from the initiator. Enforcement happens in the REST API (user search, conversation creation, message send), not just the UI.</p>
            <p>Example: allow the <code>developers</code> group to chat with anyone — source <code>GROUP:developers</code>, target <code>ANY</code>, action <code>ALLOW</code>, all capabilities checked.
               Then block one user: source <code>USER:contractor1</code>, target <code>ANY</code>, action <code>DENY</code>, higher priority.</p>

            <h4>Notifications</h4>
            <p>Web push requires HTTPS and each user enabling notifications from the chat page (bell icon). The detail level controls how much content leaves the Jira server inside push payloads — use <em>Sender only</em> or <em>Generic only</em> in privacy-sensitive environments. The test button sends a push to your own browser subscriptions.</p>

            <h4>Attachments</h4>
            <p>Disabling attachments also disables voice messages (they are stored as audio attachments). The extension allowlist further restricts the built-in safe type list — it cannot enable types that are blocked for security (executables, scripts, HTML).</p>

            <h4>Diagnostics</h4>
            <p>HTTPS must be detected for web push and voice recording to work in browsers. "VAPID configured" confirms the push signing keys exist. If pushes fail after a base URL / domain change, users need to re-enable notifications once from the chat page (subscriptions are bound to the browser origin).</p>

            <h4>Audit log</h4>
            <p>Every settings change, policy change and test notification is recorded with the acting administrator, timestamp and details — see the Overview tab.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
